cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite a measurement, a retraction or a constraint from this record, please cite it as below."
title: "logbook: the cross-project engineering record for weftspun"
abstract: >-
  The engineering record behind the weftspun projects: what was measured, what
  it cost, and which roads turned out to be dead ends. Entries record the
  measurement rather than the intention and clip the apparatus, so a result can
  be re-run rather than believed. Retractions stay in place next to what they
  retract, because a reader who knows which roads are dead ends is better off
  than one who only knows the current answer.

  Holds the working agreements every project in the workspace is written
  against (CLAUDE.md), the recurring failure modes and the guards that now
  catch them (PITFALLS.md), the running dated log (todo.md), narrative entries
  on the soft renderer's mis-scaled constants, CineForm in Godot, and the
  one-repo-per-model reranking, and the gates and apparatus that produced the
  numbers.
license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-21"
repository-code: "https://github.com/weftspun/logbook"
keywords:
  - engineering-logbook
  - reproducibility
  - retraction
  - negative-results
  - measurement
  - working-agreements
  - dataset-provenance
references:
  - type: software
    title: "ONNX: Open Neural Network Exchange"
    abstract: >-
      The operator specification the emitted graphs are written against. The
      ai.onnx domain carries 178 operators at opset 17, of which the target
      accelerator toolchain documents 58. `onnx.defs` is read directly rather
      than copied, so a second list cannot drift from it.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/onnx/onnx"
    license: Apache-2.0
  - type: software
    title: "onnxruntime-extensions"
    abstract: >-
      Where the ONNX ecosystem actually puts text processing: tokenizers and
      string operators as CUSTOM ops under the ai.onnx.contrib domain, not in
      the standard specification. Cited as the reason StringNormalizer is
      refused rather than rewritten -- a custom-op domain has no lowering to
      hardware, so text preprocessing belongs ahead of the graph rather than
      inside it.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime-extensions"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "TorchCodec: PyTorch-native media decoding"
    abstract: >-
      Decodes video, audio and images to tensors on CPU and CUDA. Cited as the
      media counterpart to the same boundary: decoding happens before the
      graph, not in it. It does not handle text, which is why it does not
      answer the string-operator question it was offered for.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/meta-pytorch/torchcodec"
    license: BSD-3-Clause
  - type: software
    title: "mathlib4"
    abstract: >-
      The library the secondary-path proofs are written against. Each exact
      rewrite in hailo_ops.usda that claims an identity is proved in
      RfdetrProofs/Secondary.lean; the six trigonometric rewrites are minimax
      polynomials and are deliberately absent, because a theorem claiming
      equality there would be false.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4"
    license: Apache-2.0
  - type: software
    title: "tinygrad"
    abstract: >-
      Reviewed for better decompositions than the hand-written ones. It
      corroborated Shrink and Hardmax exactly, and its ThresholdedRelu is worse
      for this target because it routes through Where, which the accelerator
      rejects at parse. Its real contribution was negative: the transcendental
      decompositions in codegen/decomp/transcendental.py, derived from SLEEF,
      rest on bitcast, shifts, frexp, ldexp and round-to-int, every one of
      which this target refuses. The polynomial core is plain multiply-add and
      emits; the range reduction that would make it valid outside
      [-pi/2, pi/2] does not.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/tinygrad/tinygrad"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "SLEEF: SIMD Library for Evaluating Elementary Functions"
    abstract: >-
      The source of the Payne-Hanek and Cody-Waite range reductions that
      tinygrad's transcendental decompositions implement. Cited as the
      provenance of an approach that is correct and unreachable here.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/shibatch/sleef"
    license: BSL-1.0
  - type: article
    title: "cuJSON: A Highly Parallel JSON Parser for GPUs"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Vedadi Gargary"
        given-names: Ashkan
    year: 2025
    collection-title: >-
      Proceedings of the 31st ACM International Conference on Architectural
      Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems (ASPLOS '26)
    doi: "10.1145/3760250.3762222"
    abstract: >-
      Offered as a route to string operators. It shows structural text parsing
      done data-parallel through prefix sums and precomputed structural arrays,
      and a prefix sum is CumSum, which this table already rewrites as a
      constant lower-triangular MatMul. What it does not remove is the
      obstacle: a parsed structure has a length that depends on its input, and
      data-dependent output shape is the one refusal category a static dataflow
      graph can never express. So the parallelism is available and the shape is
      not, which is why the refusal stands rather than moving.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/AutomataLab/cuJSON"
    license: MIT

